{:edb/db-before-t 1847 :edb/db-after-t 1848 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535160 50 #inst "2026-10-13T01:45:22.871Z" 13194139535160 17592186045436 true] [17592186134548 1074 "Recall that if $A=QR$ is a  QR factorization of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n$$\n{A}_{m\\times n}={Q}_{m\\times n}\\times {R}_{n\\times n},\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $Q$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\n- $R$ is an $n\\times n$ upper triangular matrix\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. $Q$ should be an $m\\times n$ matrix, so $Q$ is not necessarily a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. $Q$ has orthonormal columns and is not necessarily upper triangular.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $R$ must be an upper triangular matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535160 17592186045436 true] [17592186134548 1074 "Recall that if $A=QR$ is a  QR factorization of an $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n$$\n{A}_{m\\times n}={Q}_{m\\times n}\\times {R}_{n\\times n},\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $Q$ is an $m\\times n$ matrix whose columns form an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\n- $R$ is an $n\\times n$ upper triangular matrix\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. $Q$ should be an $m\\times n$ matrix, so $Q$ is not necessarily a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. $Q$ should be an orthogonal matrix, not necessarily an upper triangular matrix.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $R$ must be an upper triangular matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535160 17592186045436 false] [17592186148124 1074 "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=4{y}^{3}.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{y}^{3})=0\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8)=0=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-2)=0=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-2$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)=2x+7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+7)=0=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=2x+7$ gives an exact equation." 13194139535160 17592186045436 true] [17592186148124 1074 "A differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nis exact if it passes the exactness test, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're given $Q(x,y)=4{y}^{3}.$ \n\nFirst, let's differentiate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{y}^{3})=0\n$$\n\nNow, let's apply $\\frac{∂}{∂y}$ to each of the given options:\n\n- First, we check $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8)=0=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+8$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Next, we check $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-3)=0=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-3$ gives an exact equation.\n\n- Finally, we check $P(x,y)=2x+7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+7)=0=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x,y)=2x+7$ gives an exact equation." 13194139535160 17592186045436 false] [17592186152379 1074 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nw\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-2I$ maps $w$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $w$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since\n\n$$\nu\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-2I$ maps $u$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3,$ not $2.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+3I$ maps $w$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector) corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535160 17592186045436 false] [17592186152379 1074 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\nw\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-2I$ maps $w$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $w$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since\n\n$$\nu\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }v\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }w\\overset{A-2I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A-2I$ maps $u$ to $0$ in $3$ steps, \nthe vector $u$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3,$ not $2.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since\n\n$$\ny\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\ni.e. $A+3I$ maps $y$ to $0$ in $1$ step, \nthe vector $y$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ (a.k.a. an eigenvector) corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535160 17592186045436 true] [17592186152732 1074 "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **surjection** (also known as **onto**) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y.$ \n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each function.\n\n- I is a surjection. Given $y\\in (0,\\infty ),$ choose $x={log}_{3}⁡y\\in R.$ Then $f(x)=f({log}_{3}⁡y)={3}^{{log}_{3}⁡y}=y.$\n\n- II is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $2\\in Q.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)=2.$ The only number $x$ that satisfies $g(x)={x}^{2}=2$ is $x=\\sqrt{2}\\notin Q.$\n\n- III is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $h(x)={x}^{2}=-1.$  In fact, ${x}^{2}>0$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139535160 17592186045436 false] [17592186152732 1074 "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **surjection** (also known as **onto**) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y.$ \n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each function.\n\n- I is a surjection. Given $y\\in (0,\\infty ),$ choose $x={log}_{3}⁡y\\in R.$ Then $f(x)=f({log}_{3}⁡y)={3}^{{log}_{3}⁡y}=y.$\n\n- II is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $2\\in Q.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)=2.$ The only real solutions of $g(x)={x}^{2}=2$ are $x=\\pm\\sqrt{2}$, and neither belongs to $Q$.\n\n- III is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $h(x)={x}^{2}=-1.$  In fact, ${x}^{2}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in R$, with equality at $x=0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139535160 17592186045436 true] [17592186152736 1074 "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\n- I is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. Remember that the range of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is $[-1,1].$ This means that \n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSo,  for example, there is no value $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=2.$\n\n- II is *not* a bijection because it is not injective. For example, $1$ and $-1$ are distinct elements of $Q$, but $g(1)=-1=g(-1)$.\n\n- III is a bijection. First of all, it is a surjection because $∀\\,y\\in Q,$ we can choose an $x\\in Q$ such that $h(x)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(x) & =y \\\\ 8x-3 & =y \\\\ x & =\\frac{y+3}{8}\\in Q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe verify that, \n\n$$\nh(x)=h(\\frac{y+3}{8})=8(\\frac{y+3}{8})-3=y.\n$$\n\nSecond, it is an injection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h({x}_{1}) & =h({x}_{2}) \\\\ 8{x}_{1}-3 & =8{x}_{2}-3 \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $h$ is both surjective and injective, $h$ is bijective.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139535160 17592186045436 true] [17592186152736 1074 "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\n- I is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. Remember that the range of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is $[-1,1].$ This means that \n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSo,  for example, there is no value $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=2.$\n\n- II is *not* a bijection because it is not injective. Notice that $g$ has two roots, $x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.$ So, we have $g(-\\sqrt{2})=0=g(\\sqrt{2}),$ while $-\\sqrt{2}\\ne \\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- III is a bijection. First of all, it is a surjection because $∀\\,y\\in Q,$ we can choose an $x\\in Q$ such that $h(x)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(x) & =y \\\\ 8x-3 & =y \\\\ x & =\\frac{y+3}{8}\\in Q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe verify that, \n\n$$\nh(x)=h(\\frac{y+3}{8})=8(\\frac{y+3}{8})-3=y.\n$$\n\nSecond, it is an injection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h({x}_{1}) & =h({x}_{2}) \\\\ 8{x}_{1}-3 & =8{x}_{2}-3 \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $h$ is both surjective and injective, $h$ is bijective.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139535160 17592186045436 false] [17592186152767 1074 "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **surjection** (also known as **onto**) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y.$ \n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each function.\n\n- I is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $27$ from the codomain. The only number $x$ that satisfies $f(x)={3}^{x}=27$ is $x=3,$ but $3\\notin {0,1,2}.$\n\n- II is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ such that $g(x)=-1.$ The only number $x$ that satisfies $g(x)={x}^{3}=-1$ is $x=-1,$ but $-1\\notin [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- III is a surjection. Indeed, $h(0)=-1,$ $h(1)=1$ and $h(2)=3.$ So, for every $y\\in {-1,1,3},$ there exists an element $x\\in {0,1,2}$ such that $h(x)=y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139535160 17592186045436 true] [17592186152767 1074 "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **surjection** (also known as **onto**) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y.$ \n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each function.\n\n- I is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $27$ from the range. The only number $x$ that satisfies $f(x)={3}^{x}=27$ is $x=3,$ but $3\\notin {0,1,2}.$\n\n- II is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ such that $g(x)=-1.$ The only number $x$ that satisfies $g(x)={x}^{3}=-1$ is $x=-1,$ but $-1\\notin [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- III is a surjection. Indeed, $h(0)=-1,$ $h(1)=1$ and $h(2)=3.$ So, for every $y\\in {-1,1,3},$ there exists an element $x\\in {0,1,2}$ such that $h(x)=y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139535160 17592186045436 false] [17592186152823 1074 "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/66/66f4c5d8824bf692d096ed533d90c9fdcf80c3725828e9033308757a0cdaa779.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $xy+{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(xy+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions I and III equal $xy+{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function II. The $xy$ term integrates to zero over both regions by symmetry. Over $D$, the integral of $y^2$ is $\\int_{-1}^{1}(1-x^2)^3/3\\,\\mathrm{d}x=32/105$. Over $R$, it is $4\\int_{-1}^{1}y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}y=8/3$. These values are different, so function II does not satisfy the required equality.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535160 17592186045436 true] [17592186152823 1074 "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/66/66f4c5d8824bf692d096ed533d90c9fdcf80c3725828e9033308757a0cdaa779.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $xy+{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(xy+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions I and III equal $xy+{y}^{2}$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function II. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(1,1)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535160 17592186045436 false]] :edb/tempids {} :edb/committed false :edb/speculative true}
